      "id": 1,
      "text": "Roper was originally called Lee’s Mill, named for Thomas Lee, before adopting its current name in 1890.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roper%2C_North_Carolina",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Captain Thomas Blount erected a water‑powered lumber mill in the early 1700s, sparking the settlement that became Roper.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://bearolina.com/roper",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Roper’s timber once shone nationwide, with shingles and lumber barged down Kendrick’s Creek to Mackeys and shipped to distant ports.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://bearolina.com/roper",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Modern Roper focuses on agriculture, growing corn, soybeans, cotton, peanuts, tobacco, watermelons, sage, strawberries, rice, and even aquaculture.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://bearolina.com/roper",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Roper hosts the NCDA Tidewater Research Station, where North Carolina State University faculty and the Department of Agriculture conduct research.",
